{"id":"bee3e6bc-6da2-411a-a2b1-97258070df11","object":"chat.completion","created":1785002463,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    segments = case['segments']\\n    budget = case['budget']\\n    n = len(segments)\\n    # extract fields\\n    names = [s['name'] for s in segments]\\n    importances = [s['importance'] for s in segments]\\n    minimums = [s['minimum'] for s in segments]\\n    capacities = [s['capacity'] for s in segments]\\n    # feasibility check\\n    total_min = sum(minimums)\\n    total_cap = sum(capacities)\\n    if budget < total_min or budget > total_cap:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Budget out of feasible range\\\")\\n    # start at minima\\n    allocations = list(minimums)\\n    remaining = budget - total_min\\n    # allocate greedily\\n    while remaining > 0:\\n        best_idx = -1\\n        best_ratio = -1.0\\n        for i in range(n):\\n            if allocations[i] >= capacities[i]:\\n                continue\\n            # importance divided by next allocated position\\n            ratio = importances[i] / (allocations[i] + 1)\\n            if ratio > best_ratio:\\n                best_ratio = ratio\\n                best_idx = i\\n            # tie broken by original order (first encountered wins)\\n        # it must be valid because budget <= total_cap, so at least one segment has room\\n        allocations[best_idx] += 1\\n        remaining -= 1\\n    # build by_name dict (order preserved as in segments)\\n    by_name = {names[i]: allocations[i] for i in range(n)}\\n    total = sum(allocations)\\n    return {'allocations': allocations, 'by_name': by_name, 'total': total}\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) according to the specification. The task is NLP-LLM-01, which appears to be a budget allocation problem. The case object has 'segments' (list of objects with name, importance, minimum, capacity) and 'budget' (integer). The expected output is an object with \"allocations\": list of integers (presumably same order as segments), \"by_name\": dict mapping segment names to allocated amounts, and \"total\": integer (sum of allocations).\n\nThe algorithm: start with each segment at its minimum. Then while remaining budget > 0, allocate one token to the segment that maximizes importance / (current allocation + 1) or something like that? The description says: \"give one token to the segment maximizing importance divided by its next allocated position\". That sounds like for each segment, the next token would go to position (current allocation + 1). The ratio is importance / (current_allocation + 1). So we need to allocate the remaining budget one by one to the segment with highest ratio, breaking ties by original order (i.e., index in the segments list). Also, we must respect capacity: cannot exceed capacity per segment.\n\nWe must validate: budget must be at least sum of minimums, and at most sum of capacities (otherwise raise ValueError). Also budget must be integer? Probably yes.\n\nWe need to return allocations list (same order as segments), by_name dict, and total (sum of allocations, should equal budget). Also ensure no mutation of case.\n\nThe output must match schema: allocations: array of integers, by_name: object with dynamic keys from case.segments[*].name, total: integer.\n\nImplementation should be Python function solve(case) that returns dict.\n\nNow, write the code. Use standard library only.\n\nEdge cases: tie-breaking: \"Break equal priorities by original segment order.\" So when two segments have same ratio, choose the one that appears earlier in the list.\n\nAlso, \"Preserve every source identifier needed to audit the decision\" - just output the allocations and by_name.\n\nWe'll create a list of segment indices with their name, importance, capacity, minimum, and current allocation (starting at minimum). Compute total_budget = budget. Check feasibility: total_min = sum(minimums), total_cap = sum(capacities). If budget < total_min or budget > total_cap, raise ValueError. Then remaining = budget - total_min. Initialize allocations = [min_i for each segment]. Then while remaining > 0: compute ratio for each segment if current_allocation < capacity: ratio = importance / (current_allocation + 1). Else ignore (can't allocate more). Choose the segment with max ratio; tie by index. Then allocate one token: allocations[i] += 1, remaining -= 1. Continue.\n\nThen build by_name dict: {segments[i].name: allocations[i] for i}. total = sum(allocations). Return dict with \"allocations\": allocations, \"by_name\": by_name, \"total\": total.\n\nCheck that we don't mutate case: we can copy segments? We only read from case.\n\nReturn JSON-serializable: dict with list and ints.\n\nNow, produce the JSON submission with key \"implementation\" containing Python source as a JSON string. Escape properly.\n\nWrite the code as a string, then embed.\n\nMake sure to use proper indentation and no trailing spaces.\n\nI'll write the function.\n\nLet's produce the final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1496,"completion_tokens":1115,"total_tokens":2611,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1408},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":716},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1408,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":88},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}